WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:06.214
Language models get most long sums
right, but that is measured on samples,

2
00:00:06.254 --> 00:00:10.616
and the errors left are silent: a
300-digit sum, with one digit wrong.

3
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.454
Sketchpad gives a small vision-language
model, with a low-rank adapter, a sheet.

4
00:00:27.494 --> 00:00:31.267
Each forward pass reads a view of
three cells and writes a digit,

5
00:00:31.307 --> 00:00:35.140
a carry and the next operation.
Nothing else passes between steps.

6
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:58.182
So a run of any length meets only views
from a finite list: 2,446 of them.

7
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:04.961
We prove in Lean that the model is exact at every
length if and only if it is exact on those views,

8
00:01:05.001 --> 00:01:08.582
and that when steps read earlier
steps, no finite test suffices.

9
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:21.160
All 2,446 pass on a laptop GPU, in a
browser, each by at least three logits.

10
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:27.778
On 220 pre-registered sums and
products, it answered all 220;

11
00:01:27.818 --> 00:01:31.243
the strongest of ten other models, 217.

12
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.961
Certified procedures compose:

13
00:01:35.001 --> 00:01:40.549
an algebra runtime factors and solves polynomials
with every number computed by the model.

14
00:01:40.589 --> 00:01:44.585
A dialogue adapter, trained with
on-policy distillation and GRPO,

15
00:01:44.625 --> 00:01:49.739
opens the sheet when it needs exact
arithmetic; at gate zero, it is the base model,

16
00:01:49.779 --> 00:01:50.484
bit for bit.

17
00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:02.058
Inside, a multiplication digit is decided
in the fifth full-attention layer:

18
00:02:02.098 --> 00:02:07.628
a push after it changes every digit
tested; a push up to depth fifteen, none.

19
00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:18.672
Sketchpad runs in your browser, and
rechecks its weakest views on your GPU.

20
00:02:18.712 --> 00:02:20.777
The paper, code and model are open.
